Hi [~accountid:712020:9a6891b5-78f1-463d-aef5-f39379a2398b] 

I was able to have a look at the document. Lets reiterate and summarise again:

* Google wants to receive additional data about our drivers; it will be used for the same purpose as the more limited scope of data we currently collect - for conversion analysts;
* the data is received from drivers who have consented to be tracked before via our Cookie Banner on the website; however, their consent concerns the placement of cookies - and in this case, we are not sharing cookies but first party data (email, phone number, etc). Drivers did not consent previously to the use of this data (gap:red_square: ) and were not informed about the activity via the privacy notice (gap:red_square: ).
* the data would be hashed (anonymised for Google) before it is being shared. However, I found in the [doc|https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/13258081?hl=en] that later this data will be matched by Google with the data about drivers they currently have, which means - this data is pseudonymised, not anonymised, so the GDPR and privacy laws would still apply to the transfer.

What we currently want:

* we want to understand if it is ok to share this data with Google to enhance our analytics;

{panel:bgColor=#fffae6}
*Privacy opinion (preliminary):*

* if you can confirm that the above is correct, *then we cannot lawfully transfer this data to Google yet*;
* to make it lawful, we need to ensure that the drivers are informed about this via our updated privacy notice; additionally, we will have to ensure internal audit trail and a lawful basis (consent likely not strictly necessary), we could possibly rely on legitimate interest.
* privacy notices will be updated by EOY, as per my information.
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#eae6ff}
However, because:

* the activity is already ongoing for other verticals (business);
* amount of data is very limited and it is pseudonymised in transit, which reduces privacy risks;
* the activity is important for business and will allow us to basically make more money (with improved analytics);
* privacy notice for drivers is going to be updated soon, and includes basic provisions on the topic on analytics;

the risks of actually going forward with the activity should be fairly minimal, but any business decisions will have to be signed off by the leadership team. Additionally, this will have to be flagged to the DPO so they are aware of the risk.
{panel}

Next steps:

* please confirm the summary for me and your understanding of the privacy risks;
* this will be flagged to the DPO;
* if the leadership team still wants to proceed despite the risks, they could sign off the activity.

however, instead of looking at extreme scenarios where we would decide to go 100% preauth level in a country or not, we should try to optimize the metrics. 

I’ve decided to check the numbers for certain groups of users:

* users where their total_captured_sum_eur is below 10 euros;
* users where their total_captured_sum_eur is between 10 and 100 euros;
* users where their total_captured_sum_eur is between 100 and 1000 euros;
* users where their total_captured_sum_eur is above 1000 euros;
* orders where the predicted price is below 10 euros;
* orders where the predicted price is between 10 and 20 euros;
* orders where the predicted price is between 20 and 30 euros;
* orders where the predicted price is between 30 and 40 euros;
* orders where the predicted price is between 40 and 50 euros;
* orders where the predicted price is between 50 and 100 euros;
* orders where the predicted price is more than 100 euros;

for each of these groups, I’ve checked the debt recovery and compared it to the recovery of the rest of the orders that don’t have preauth in the same country. 

for example, in RO, overall recovery is at 93.9%. however, users that have their total_captured_sum_eur below 10 euros have their recovery at 71%. whereas users that have more than 1000 in their total_captured_sum_eur have a recovery of 98.5%. therefore, there’s an opportunity to target specifically the group of users that have their total_captured_sum_eur below 10 euros. 

other relevant metrics that are in the sheet: 

* *avg_price_increase_when_price_changes*: shows on avg how much the final price increases vs the prediction. the smaller the increase, the higher the chances we’ll capture if there’s a preauthorization on the order. 
* *preauth_ratio*: it’s the country’s current levels of preauth. the higher the level is, the more freely we can consider doing more preauths, as users will be already used to it. this metric is related to the concern around the increase of CS costs for users that aren’t familiar with preauthorizations.
* *conversion*: rate of how many order requests convert to GMV-producing orders. this metric is related to the worry about payment cost increases if we do preauths and orders don’t happen, we’ll get extra costs we didn’t have before. 
* *adyen_ratio*: shows how much of the country volume goes through Adyen. this is relevant in regards to having incremental preauthorizations, that increase our chances of capturing payments fully. 

the tab *_all country numbers_* of the sheet has all the metrics for July/2024 data: [https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/18v_OnyknnNbaIIj3dDIbn18zSd5F8h5328-QX5bvICk/edit?usp=sharing|https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/18v_OnyknnNbaIIj3dDIbn18zSd5F8h5328-QX5bvICk/edit?usp=sharing|smart-link]

